<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
  <p class="font-bold">A score of 99.901% accuracy can hide a flood of false alarms.</p>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base">
    <div class="border-2 border-ink p-3">
      <strong>1,000,000 cases</strong>
      <p>Only 10 are truly positive.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="border-2 border-ink p-3">
      <strong>1,000 alarms</strong>
      <p>10 real and 990 false.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 300 142"
    class="w-full max-w-sm"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="accuracy-title accuracy-desc"
  >
    <title id="accuracy-title">Nearly all predictions correct but only one percent of alarms correct</title>
    <desc id="accuracy-desc">
      One thousand flagged alarms include ten true cases and nine hundred ninety false alarms.
    </desc>
    <rect x="10" y="32" width="280" height="30" fill="#bd4b37" fill-opacity=".55" />
    <rect x="10" y="32" width="2.8" height="30" fill="#387a91" />
    <g fill="currentColor" font-size="16">
      <text x="10" y="22">
        Among the 1,000 alarms
      </text>
      <text x="10" y="84">
        10 true
      </text>
      <text x="290" y="84" text-anchor="end">
        990 false
      </text>
      <text x="10" y="112" font-weight="bold">
        Precision = 1%, despite
      </text>
      <text x="10" y="134" font-weight="bold">
        99.901% overall accuracy
      </text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">
    The example catches all 10 rare cases (100% recall) and correctly labels 999,010 of
    1,000,000 cases (99.901% accuracy). But only 10 of the 1,000 alarms are real. Counting all
    easy negatives makes accuracy look spectacular while alarms remain unreliable.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
